<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scottish Highlands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Highlands of Scotland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Scottish Highlands are the mountainous north and west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, beyond the Highland Boundary Fault. Home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> peoples recorded by Ptolemy and later to the Gaelic-speaking clans, they kept a distinct culture into modern times. Evictions during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/highland-clearances">Highland Clearances</a> of the 18th and 19th centuries sent many Highlanders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-edward-island">Prince Edward Island</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands.jpg" alt="Scottish Highlands" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jrockley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scottish_Highlands_and_Lowlands.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Highlands are the northernmost mountain ranges of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, divided by the Great Glen between the Grampian Mountains to the south-east and the Northwest Highlands. The name also covers the area north and west of the Highland Boundary Fault, which runs from Arran to Stonehaven, though the exact boundary is not clearly defined, particularly in the east. The region lies on ancient Cambrian and Precambrian rocks uplifted in the Caledonian Orogeny, with Lewisian gneiss up to 3 billion years old, and includes Ben Nevis, the highest mountain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-isles">British Isles</a>. The Highlands are the only area in the British Isles with a boreal forest biome, with remnants of the Caledonian Forest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region was home to the Caledonians or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a> and became the mainland stronghold of the Hebridean Gaels, whose Gaelic language succeeded that of their neighbours. The cultural distinction between Highlander and Lowlander was first noted towards the end of the 14th century. The clan was the main social unit, and James VI saw clans as a challenge to royal authority. The Statutes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iona">Iona</a> in 1609 began integrating clan leaders into Scottish society. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a> the British government banned the bearing of arms and the wearing of tartan, laws mostly repealed by the end of the 18th century. The Black Watch, raised in 1739, was the first Highland regiment of the British Army.</p><p>Between about 1760 and 1850 agricultural improvement brought the Highland clearances, in which many tenants were evicted to make way for large-scale sheep farms; in the west and north they were resettled in crofting communities and worked in kelping and fishing. Famine recurred, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> blight arrived in 1846. The population, around 300,000 in the 18th and early 19th centuries, then declined through emigration, mostly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Crofters, many in the Free Church after 1843, challenged landlords through the Highland Land League in the 1880s, and the Crofters Holdings (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>) Act 1886 reduced rents and guaranteed fixity of tenure.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a> is regarded as the capital of the Highlands, and the Highland Council has its administrative centre there. The area is among the most sparsely populated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Scottish Gaelic, once the language of the region, is now largely confined to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a>, and a Highland form of Scottish English predominates. Tartan and the kilt were adopted by elites in the 1820s after the Ossian cycle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>'s staging of George IV's visit in 1822, and tartan became a major symbol of Scottish identity.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Scottish Highlands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many anthropologists class as chiefdoms the Germanic peoples who took over the western Roman Empire in the 5th century, with their kings, warrior aristocracy, free men, serfs and slaves, and in the <b>Scottish Highlands</b> clan chiefs kept courts of their own until the Heritable Jurisdictions Act of 1746.</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carnonacae appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 on the western coast of the <b>Scottish Highlands</b>, north of the Creones and south of the Caereni, roughly in modern Wester Ross.</blockquote><a href="/state/carnonacae">Carnonacae</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on farming, fishing and shipbuilding, with many settlers from the <b>Scottish Highlands</b>, Ireland and Acadia.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-prince-edward-island">Colony of Prince Edward Island</a></li></ul></section>
